t substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4-10t \\\\ y=-1+6t \\\\ z=7-7t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d275a218-aa12-54bd-a4ff-4be892226f4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3199e0-7ca6-5317-9afa-2d99c3b3771e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a3199e0-7ca6-5317-9afa-2d99c3b3771e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d7476b-5763-583f-adf2-eca36812c205" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121558} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58069a6a-b603-54fd-bc70-e51cd1dd4dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58069a6a-b603-54fd-bc70-e51cd1dd4dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357317} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0572da-5a04-5fb8-88a9-093250d4d10a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca21829b-f75e-5a9d-97e2-6ce18378c4e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-236986" :question/problem "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-5,7,1)$ and $Q(-4,-1,6).$\n\n| $x=$ | {{field-1}} | $t\\in [$ {{field-2}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | $7-8t$ |  |\n| $z=$ | $1+5t$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -8t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5+t \\\\ 7-8t \\\\ 1+5t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-5+t \\\\ y=7-8t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d275a218-aa12-54bd-a4ff-4be892226f4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a025e7-22f9-55a7-bbec-9097a8473a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314843} {:answer/id #uuid "1aace4d2-f6bb-5a1c-932a-7999444d7f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 